iommu/arm-smmu-qcom: keep SM8450 MDSS in an identity domain

The bootloader leaves the SM8450 display controller scanning out its
splash buffer. MDSS is absent from qcom_smmu_client_of_match, so the
device gets a translating default domain as soon as it joins its IOMMU
group and every fetch faults until the msm driver resets the block:

  platform ae00000.display-subsystem: Adding to iommu group 6
  arm-smmu 15000000.iommu: Unhandled context fault: fsr=0x402,
    iova=0xb8e41200, fsynr=0x700021, cbfrsynra=0x2800, cb=5

On the Galaxy Tab S8 Ultra this cuts the bootloader's command-mode
scanout off mid-frame while simpledrm is still drawing the console. Add
the compatible, as for the other MDSS generations, so the bootloader
mapping survives until msm attaches its own domain.

Generated from the pinned Linux 7.2 source. Not yet submitted upstream.

--- a/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/arm/arm-smmu/arm-smmu-qcom.c
@@ -425,6 +425,7 @@
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,sm6375-mdss" },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,sm8150-mdss" },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,sm8250-mdss" },
+	{ .compatible = "qcom,sm8450-mdss" },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,x1e80100-mdss" },
 	{ }
 };
